function economyTopic(){
  // ON THE ANATOMY (Fable build spec 2026-08-01). The letter's conditional prose
  // is gone: readings live in the slot reads, mechanism lives in the walkthrough,
  // judgment lives in My view and changes by revision, never by ternary.
  const inf=s('banxico-inflacion'),core=s('banxico-inflacion-subyacente'),rateRaw=s('banxico-tasa-objetivo'),igae=s('banxico-igae'),gdp=s('banxico-pib-crecimiento'),peso=s('banxico-usdmxn-fix'),real=realWage(),W=last(monthlyLast(s('banxico-salario-minimo')));
  const I=last(inf),Ic=last(core),R=last(rateRaw),G=last(igae),GDP=last(gdp),P=last(peso),RW=last(real);
  if(!I||!Ic||!R||!G||!GDP||!P||!RW||!W)throw new Error('Economy inputs are incomplete');
  const baseYear=dateObj(real[0].date).getUTCFullYear();
  const realGap=R.value-I.value;
  const line=(name,points,color,format)=>({name,points,color,format});
  const rateMonthly=monthlyLast(rateRaw);
  const REVISED='Aug 1, 2026';
  return {
    anatomy:true,
    title:'Economy & money',
    stamp:`newest number ${shortDate(P.date)}, ${dateObj(P.date).getUTCFullYear()}`,
    lede:`Mexico's central bank, Banxico, lends pesos overnight at ${fmt(R.value,2)}% while consumer prices are rising ${fmt(I.value,1)}% a year, so borrowing pesos costs roughly ${fmt(realGap,1)} points more than prices are climbing. That gap sets what a Mexican company pays for a loan, and it pays a foreign investor to hold pesos rather than dollars, which is what moves the peso. Growth runs on a slower clock, at ${fmt(GDP.value,1)}% in ${quarterLabel(GDP.date)}. The wage floor runs on its own clock: a government commission raises it by decree, and it stands at MX$${fmt(W.value,2)} a day.`,
    howItWorks:{
      revised:REVISED,
      paras:[
        `Banxico has one main tool: the interest rate it charges banks to borrow overnight, which is ${fmt(R.value,2)}% today. Its job is to keep consumer prices rising at about 3% a year, and it treats anything between 2 and 4% as close enough. When inflation runs above that band the rate goes up, which does two separate things: loans get dearer, so households and companies borrow and spend less, and peso savings pay more, which pulls money toward pesos. When inflation falls back inside the band the rate comes down and both effects run in reverse.`,
        `The peso answers to that rate before it answers to the economy. An investor deciding whether to park money in pesos or in dollars compares what each one pays, and the wider the peso's advantage, the more money is willing to sit in pesos. So the currency can hold up in a weak year and slip in a strong one. These charts have a blind spot there. The dollar half of that comparison is set by the US Federal Reserve and is not plotted on this page, so what you can see here is what Mexico did to its own rate, not what happened to the gap between the two.`,
        `Growth is reported twice, and the two numbers are not interchangeable. IGAE, the monthly activity indicator, is assembled from partial data, comes out first, and gets revised afterward. The quarterly national accounts from INEGI, the national statistics agency, land months later and settle the record. Today the monthly indicator reads ${signed(G.value,1,'%')} for ${dateLabel(G.date)} and the quarterly figure reads ${fmt(GDP.value,1)}% for ${quarterLabel(GDP.date)}. Those two cover different periods, so they are not in conflict here, but when they do disagree about the same period the quarterly number is the one that stands.`,
        `The general minimum wage is MX$${fmt(W.value,2)} a day, and it works differently from everything above. A commission called CONASAMI sets it by decree, and it steps up in January rather than moving with supply and demand. After adjusting for prices, a day at the minimum buys about ${fmt(RW.value/100,1)} times what it bought in ${baseYear}. It is the legal minimum, so it tells you nothing about what a typical Mexican earns. It bites where pay already sits close to it: formal jobs at the bottom of the scale, and services whose main cost is people, such as kitchens, cleaning and care work. Raise the floor and those prices follow.`,
        `The numbers below do not share an observation date. Inflation is current to the month, growth to the quarter, and the policy rate and the peso to the trading day. So the peso quote from ${shortDate(P.date)} and the activity reading for ${dateLabel(G.date)} are describing different weeks of the same economy, and lining them up as if they were simultaneous is the easiest mistake to make on this page.`
      ]
    },
    slots:[
      {chart:{title:'Inflation and the policy rate, percent',stat:`${fmt(R.value,2)}%`,series:[line('Headline inflation',inf.slice(-72),GREEN,v=>fmt(v,1)+'%'),line('Policy rate',rateMonthly.slice(-72),INK,v=>fmt(v,2)+'%')],yFormat:v=>fmt(v,1)+'%',band:[2,4],label:'Headline inflation and the Banxico policy rate',srcId:'banxico-inflacion',srcId2:'banxico-tasa-objetivo'},
       read:`Headline inflation ran ${fmt(I.value,1)}% in the year to ${dateLabel(I.date)}, against a policy rate of ${fmt(R.value,2)}%. The ${fmt(realGap,1)} points between them are the real cost of peso money: a Mexican company borrows at the policy rate plus whatever its bank adds, and pays that much above the rate at which prices in general are rising. Core inflation, which leaves out food and energy, was ${fmt(Ic.value,1)}% in ${dateLabel(Ic.date)}. Core is the better guide to where the rate goes next, because food and fuel prices move on harvests, weather and oil, which no Mexican interest rate can reach. The shaded band is Banxico's 2 to 4% tolerance.`},
      {chart:{title:'Economic activity, annual percent change by month',stat:`${signed(G.value,1,'%')}`,series:[line('IGAE annual change',igae.slice(-72),GREEN,v=>fmt(v,1)+'%')],yFormat:v=>fmt(v,1)+'%',label:'IGAE annual percentage change by month',srcId:'banxico-igae'},
       read:`The monthly activity indicator was ${signed(G.value,1,'%')} in ${dateLabel(G.date)} against the same month a year earlier. The quarterly national accounts, which settle the record, put growth at ${fmt(GDP.value,1)}% for ${quarterLabel(GDP.date)}, after ${fmt(prior(gdp).value,1)}% in ${quarterLabel(prior(gdp).date)}. Both are comparisons with a year earlier, not with the quarter before. Growth at these levels means a Mexican company is not being carried along by the market it sells into, so its revenue has to come from taking share, from raising prices, or from exporting.`},
      {chart:{title:'Pesos per US dollar, daily, one year',stat:fmt(P.value,2),series:[line('MXN per USD',peso.slice(-260),GREEN,v=>fmt(v,2))],yFormat:v=>fmt(v,1),label:'Peso per dollar FIX rate, one year',srcId:'banxico-usdmxn-fix'},
       read:`A dollar bought ${fmt(P.value,2)} pesos on ${shortDate(P.date)}. This chart opens at ${fmt(peso[0].value,2)} pesos in ${dateLabel(peso[0].date)}, one year back. A lower number means a stronger peso, and everyone on a different side of it feels the same move differently: imported machinery and dollar debts get cheaper for a Mexican company, Mexican goods get dearer for the American buying them, and a remittance sent home from abroad converts into fewer pesos at the grocery store. A stronger peso also raises the dollar price of a Mexican business, because the same peso valuation converts into more dollars.`},
      {chart:{title:`Minimum wage adjusted for prices, ${baseYear} = 100`,stat:fmt(RW.value,0),series:[line('Real minimum wage',real,GREEN,v=>fmt(v,0))],yFormat:v=>fmt(v,0),label:'Inflation-adjusted general minimum wage index',srcId:'banxico-salario-minimo',srcId2:'banxico-inpc'},
       read:`The general minimum wage is MX$${fmt(W.value,2)} a day, and adjusted for prices it stands at ${fmt(RW.value,0)} against ${baseYear} = 100. That line is set at CONASAMI by decree rather than between an employer and a worker, so it is one cost inside a Mexican business that moves on a political calendar instead of a market one. Anyone pricing a company whose payroll sits near the floor, in retail, kitchens, cleaning or care, is buying that calendar along with it.`}
    ],
    quarter:[
      `Banxico cut its policy rate again inside the quarter, in May, extending a year of easing that has taken the overnight rate from ${fmt(rateRaw[0].value,2)}% in ${dateLabel(rateRaw[0].date)} to ${fmt(R.value,2)}%. Headline inflation, ${fmt(I.value,1)}% in the year to ${dateLabel(I.date)}, came down over those three months faster than core inflation, which was ${fmt(Ic.value,1)}% in ${dateLabel(Ic.date)}. Most of the fall therefore sits in food and energy rather than in the underlying trend.`,
      `The wage floor held at MX$${fmt(W.value,2)} a day, because it is reset by decree in January and not inside a quarter. Growth stayed slow: ${fmt(GDP.value,1)}% for ${quarterLabel(GDP.date)} in the quarterly accounts, and ${signed(G.value,1,'%')} for ${dateLabel(G.date)} in the monthly indicator.`
    ],
    ahead:eventMatches(/CPI|INPC|IGAE|GDP|Banxico monetary|expectations|employment/i,5),
    view:{
      paras:[
        `I think one number explains most of this page: peso money costs ${fmt(realGap,1)} points more than prices are rising, with the rate at ${fmt(R.value,2)}% and inflation at ${fmt(I.value,1)}%. That is what pays a foreigner to hold pesos, and it is what makes a Mexican company think twice about borrowing to expand. The natural reading of a firm peso is confidence in Mexico, and these numbers do not support that reading as far as I can tell: a dollar bought ${fmt(peso[0].value,2)} pesos in ${dateLabel(peso[0].date)} and ${fmt(P.value,2)} on ${shortDate(P.date)}, over a stretch when the economy grew ${fmt(GDP.value,1)}% in ${quarterLabel(GDP.date)}. What I cannot do from here is separate the two halves of that gap, because this page plots the Mexican rate and not the dollar one.`,
        `I expect the rate to keep coming down in small steps rather than stopping. Banxico has moved gradually all year, from ${fmt(rateRaw[0].value,2)}% in ${dateLabel(rateRaw[0].date)} to ${fmt(R.value,2)}%, and core inflation at ${fmt(Ic.value,1)}% gives it no reason to hurry. I expect growth to stay close to where it is while that happens, because cheaper credit takes several quarters to reach borrowing and hiring. Two of the numbers that price a purchase of a Mexican company are on this page: the policy rate, which sets the cost of the peso debt already inside it, and the peso on the day the money converts, which sets what the equity costs in dollars. The wage floor is the third, if the payroll sits near it.`
      ],
      changeMind:`the peso holding its range through another year of cuts, which would tell me it is trading on something other than the gap between peso and dollar rates, or core inflation rising in two consecutive monthly CPI releases from INEGI while Banxico keeps cutting, which would tell me the easing has run ahead of the inflation data. Both tests are on the calendar above: the next policy decision and the next CPI print are dated there.`
    },
    record:changedMatches('economy',3),
    sources:[topicSource('banxico-inflacion'),topicSource('banxico-inflacion-subyacente'),topicSource('banxico-tasa-objetivo'),topicSource('banxico-igae'),topicSource('banxico-pib-crecimiento'),topicSource('banxico-usdmxn-fix'),topicSource('banxico-salario-minimo'),topicSource('banxico-inpc')],
    method:`Every reading keeps its own observation date, and they are not the same date. Core inflation is charted on the Numbers page; here it appears once, inside the first read, as a check on the headline. The real minimum wage is the decreed daily wage deflated by the consumer price index and indexed to ${baseYear}. The walkthrough above carries its revision date on the page.`
  };
}

function paymentsTopic(){
  // FIRST PAGE ON THE NEW ANATOMY (Fable ruling 2026-08-01). The walkthrough teaches
  // the rails; the slots carry the dated numbers with a read each; the quarter module
  // says what moved; the view is labeled and falsifiable. Counts lead throughout,
  // because peso totals are dominated by large corporate transfers.
  const spei=s('banxico-spei-operaciones'),codi=s('banxico-codi-operaciones'),debitOps=s('banxico-tpv-debito-ops'),creditOps=s('banxico-tpv-credito-ops'),ecomOps=s('banxico-ecommerce-ops'),circ=s('banxico-circulante');
  const cardOps=sumShared(debitOps,creditOps),S=last(spei),CoDi=last(codi),Card=last(cardOps),Debit=debitOps.find(p=>p.date===Card?.date),E=last(ecomOps),C=last(circ),CYr=yearAgo(circ);
  // Year-ago comparisons are hoisted and required rather than guarded inside the prose.
  // The smoke test forbids a conditional in the two judgment fields (Fable: judgment
  // changes by revision, not by ternary), and it is right to. A paragraph that silently
  // swaps its argument when a lookup misses is two different judgments wearing one
  // sentence. If a comparison is genuinely absent the page takes its documented failure
  // state instead.
  const CardYr=yearAgo(cardOps,Card),DebitYr=yearAgo(debitOps,Debit),CoDiYr=yearAgo(codi);
  if(!S||!CoDi||!Card||!Debit||!E||!C||!CYr||!CardYr||!DebitYr||!CoDiYr)throw new Error('Payments inputs are incomplete');
  const FACTS={enifCashSmall:{v:85.2,label:"INEGI's 2024 survey"},enifUrl:'https://inegi.org.mx/contenidos/programas/enif/2024/doc/enif_2024_resultados.pdf'};
  const debitReview=(meta('banxico-tpv-debito-monto').flags||[]).some(f=>String(f).startsWith('series_jump_'));
  const debitShare=Debit.value/Card.value*100;
  const perDay=S.value/new Date(Date.UTC(dateObj(S.date).getUTCFullYear(),dateObj(S.date).getUTCMonth()+1,0)).getUTCDate();
  const codiRatio=(()=>{const m=spei.find(p=>p.date===CoDi.date);return m?Math.round(m.value/CoDi.value):null;})();
  const storeRatio=(()=>{const c=cardOps.find(p=>p.date===E.date);return c?c.value/E.value:null;})();
  const tCirc=trendWord(C.value,CYr.value);
  const line=(name,points,color,format)=>({name,points,color,format});
  const newest=[S.date,C.date].sort().at(-1);
  const REVISED='Aug 1, 2026';
  return {
    anatomy:true,
    title:'Payments & fintech',
    stamp:`newest number ${dateLabel(newest)}`,
    lede:`The amount of cash in circulation in Mexico is ${fmt(pct(C.value,circ[0].value),0)}% higher than it was in ${dateLabel(circ[0].date)}, measured in current pesos, and it is still rising. Bank transfers and card payments grew over those same years. Digital payment in Mexico has so far been adding new occasions to pay rather than taking them away from cash. The numbers below cover the cash stock, the bank-transfer rail and the card rail, each with its own observation date.`,
    howItWorks:{
      revised:REVISED,
      paras:[
        `Start with cash, because most Mexicans still do. <b>${fmt(FACTS.enifCashSmall.v,1)}%</b> of adults told ${FACTS.enifCashSmall.label} they pay cash for everyday purchases, and the total value of notes and coins in the country has gone up since that survey. Every digital payment product sold in Mexico is competing against that habit, which is why the cash figures on this page carry as much weight as the card figures.`,
        `Digital money moves on two rails. The first is SPEI, the bank-to-bank transfer system: it settles in seconds, costs the sender nothing at most banks, and is what a Mexican means by <i>transferencia</i>. Banxico, Mexico's central bank, owns it and runs it. The second rail is cards. An acquirer rents the terminal to the merchant, routes the authorisation to the card network and takes a fee out of every sale, and that rail belongs to the banks and the international card networks rather than to the state. One rail is free to the person paying and the other takes a cut of every sale, which is the main reason the two rails have grown at different speeds.`,
        `Around those rails sits the industry. Deposit accounts are concentrated in a small group of large banks, and the CNBV, the banking and securities regulator, is where accounts by institution are counted. Since the 2018 Ley Fintech, non-bank firms can hold a licence of their own, either as electronic payment institutions, which hold customer balances and move them, or as crowdfunding institutions, which match lenders with borrowers. Alongside them sit the acquirers that put card terminals into small merchants' hands. Supervision is split down the middle: the CNBV grants and polices the licences, while Banxico owns the rails and sets the rules for what moving money costs.`,
        `A card sale leaves a record. The acquirer sees it, the bank sees it, and the tax authority can ask for it, so a merchant who is not registered, or who is registered for less than he actually sells, treats accepting cards as a tax decision before it is ever a pricing decision. The same problem runs on the other side of the counter, because a worker paid in cash has to deposit that cash somewhere before any of it can be spent digitally. Those two facts set the ceiling on how fast card acceptance can grow in Mexico, and no checkout design moves either of them.`,
        `One rule for reading the counts below. Count payments, not pesos. A handful of very large corporate transfers is enough to dominate the peso totals, so the value of transfers tells you what treasury departments did that month, while the count tells you about payroll, rent, and the taco stand that takes a transferencia.`
      ]
    },
    slots:[
      {chart:{title:'Notes and coins in circulation, MX$ trillions',stat:`MX$${fmt(C.value/1e6,2)}tn`,series:[line('Currency in circulation',circ.slice(-72),GREEN,v=>'MX$'+fmt(v/1e6,2)+'tn')],yFormat:v=>'MX$'+fmt(v/1e6,1)+'tn',label:'Notes and coins in circulation, monthly stock',srcId:'banxico-circulante'},
       read:`Mexicans held MX$${fmt(C.value/1e6,2)} trillion in notes and coins in ${dateLabel(C.date)}${tCirc.known&&Math.abs(tCirc.pct)>=0.5?`, ${fmt(Math.abs(tCirc.pct),1)}% ${tCirc.pct>0?'more':'less'} than a year earlier`:''}. Two cautions on what that counts. It is cash people are holding, not cash they spent, and it is in current pesos, so part of any rise is higher prices rather than more notes changing hands. Even allowing for both, the direction has not turned: this line has kept climbing through the same years in which transfers and card purchases climbed.`},
      {chart:{title:'SPEI transfers per month, millions',stat:`${fmt(S.value/1e6,0)}m`,series:[line('SPEI transfers',spei.slice(-72),GREEN,v=>fmt(v/1e6,0)+'m')],yFormat:v=>fmt(v/1e6,0)+'m',label:'SPEI monthly transfer count',srcId:'banxico-spei-operaciones'},
       read:`SPEI carried ${fmt(S.value/1e6,0)} million transfers in ${dateLabel(S.date)}, about ${fmt(perDay/1e6,1)} million a day. Card terminals across the whole country averaged ${fmt(Card.value/3/1e6,0)} million purchases a month in ${quarterLabel(Card.date)}, so the free transfer rail moves more payments than the priced card rail does. A transfer is not the same thing as a purchase, though. Payroll, rent, invoices between companies and money a person moves between his own accounts all land in this count, so read it as how much money moves digitally rather than as how much shopping is done digitally.`},
      {chart:{title:'Card purchases at terminals, debit vs credit, millions per quarter',stat:`${fmt(Debit.value/1e6,0)}m debit`,series:[line('Debit',debitOps.slice(-24),GREEN,v=>fmt(v/1e6,0)+'m'),line('Credit',creditOps.slice(-24),INK,v=>fmt(v/1e6,0)+'m')],yFormat:v=>fmt(v/1e6,0)+'m',label:'Quarterly debit and credit card purchases',srcId:'banxico-tpv-debito-ops',srcId2:'banxico-tpv-credito-ops'},
       read:`Terminals handled ${fmt(Card.value/1e9,2)} billion card purchases in ${quarterLabel(Card.date)}, and ${fmt(debitShare,1)}% of them were on debit cards${debitOps.find(p=>p.date===cardOps[0].date)?`, against ${fmt(debitOps.find(p=>p.date===cardOps[0].date).value/cardOps[0].value*100,1)}% in ${quarterLabel(cardOps[0].date)}`:''}. A debit purchase is money already sitting in the account rather than credit extended, and five years of card growth have barely changed that mix, so the ordinary Mexican card transaction is still someone spending a balance he already has.`},
      {table:{cols:['Rail','Latest','Period','Source'],rows:[
        ['SPEI transfers',`${fmt(S.value/1e6,0)}m`,dateLabel(S.date),'Banxico'],
        ['CoDi payments',`${fmt(CoDi.value/1e3,0)}k`,dateLabel(CoDi.date),'Banxico'],
        ['Card purchases at terminals',`${fmt(Card.value/1e9,2)}bn`,quarterLabel(Card.date),'Banxico'],
        ['Card purchases online',`${fmt(E.value/1e6,0)}m`,quarterLabel(E.date),'Banxico']
      ]},
       read:`CoDi is the central bank's QR-code payment product, and it is built on top of SPEI. In ${dateLabel(CoDi.date)} it handled roughly one payment for every ${codiRatio?fmt(codiRatio,0):'several thousand'} ordinary SPEI transfers. Paying by QR saves a Mexican almost nothing, because the plain transfer underneath it is already instant and free at most banks, so CoDi arrived with no cost and no delay left to remove.${storeRatio?` Online card payments are the opposite case. Shoppers still make about ${fmt(storeRatio,1)} card purchases at a physical terminal for every one online${ecomOps.find(p=>p.date===cardOps[0].date)?`, against ${fmt(cardOps[0].value/ecomOps.find(p=>p.date===cardOps[0].date).value,1)} to one in ${quarterLabel(cardOps[0].date)}`:''}, so online is taking share of card spending steadily rather than suddenly.`:''}`}
    ],
    quarter:[
      `The movement this quarter was inside the card number rather than in the totals. Card purchases at terminals in ${quarterLabel(Card.date)}, the newest quarter Banxico has published, were up ${fmt(pct(Card.value,CardYr.value),1)}% on ${quarterLabel(CardYr.date)}, and the growth was lopsided. Against that same quarter a year earlier, purchases charged to credit cards rose ${fmt(pct(Card.value-Debit.value,CardYr.value-DebitYr.value),1)}% while purchases on debit cards rose ${fmt(pct(Debit.value,DebitYr.value),1)}%. A purchase on a credit card is a payment and a small loan at the same time, so a growing share of the extra spending at the counter is being funded by a credit line rather than by money already in the account.`,
      `The relationship between cash and digital did not change. Notes and coins in circulation kept rising while the transfer count and the card count rose, which is the same pattern both sides have shown since 2020. CoDi handled ${fmt(CoDi.value/1e3,0)} thousand payments in ${dateLabel(CoDi.date)}, ${signed(pct(CoDi.value,CoDiYr.value),1,'%')} against the same month a year earlier, so the QR rail is not filling in behind cash either.`
    ],
    ahead:[
      {date:'Monthly',label:'SPEI transfer count and CoDi payment count',source:'Banxico · release date varies'},
      {date:'Quarterly',label:'Card purchases at terminals and online, debit and credit reported separately',source:'Banxico · release date varies'},
      {date:'Monthly',label:'Notes and coins in circulation, the test for cash substitution',source:'Banxico · release date varies'},
      {date:'Oct 2026',label:'Next full review of this section',source:'The Mexico Brief',approx:true}
    ],
    view:{
      paras:[
        `What I take from these two series is that digital payment in Mexico is still adding occasions to pay rather than taking them away from cash. Terminals handled ${fmt(Card.value/1e9,2)} billion card purchases in ${quarterLabel(Card.date)} and Mexicans were holding MX$${fmt(C.value/1e6,2)} trillion in notes and coins in ${dateLabel(C.date)}, and both counts are higher than they were a year before. Real substitution would show up as the cash line flattening while the card count keeps climbing, and nothing like that has appeared in these series. So when someone tells me Mexico is going cashless, the series I check first is the one about cash.`,
        `My guess is that credit purchases keep growing faster than debit purchases while the cash stock also keeps growing. That combination means more of the extra spending at the counter is funded by credit lines than by balances people already hold, and this page cannot tell you whether those balances are being paid off, because it counts payments and not lending. Banxico publishes the credit-card lending series that would answer it. A buyer of a Mexican retailer, or a lender to its customers, inherits one number that no card-growth story answers: the share of sales that still arrives in cash, because that share sets what the accounts can prove and what a lender can verify about the buyer.`
      ],
      changeMind:`notes and coins in circulation flattening or falling for two consecutive quarters in Banxico's monthly currency series while the quarterly card count keeps rising. That pattern would be substitution rather than addition, and it has not appeared yet. Currency is published monthly and the card counts quarterly, so confirming a turn takes two more card releases.`
    },
    record:changedMatches('payments',3),
    sources:[topicSource('banxico-spei-operaciones'),topicSource('banxico-codi-operaciones'),topicSource('banxico-tpv-debito-ops'),topicSource('banxico-tpv-credito-ops'),topicSource('banxico-ecommerce-ops'),topicSource('banxico-circulante'),{label:'INEGI · ENIF 2024',href:FACTS.enifUrl,date:'2024'},{label:'CNBV · financial inclusion database 2024',href:'https://pnif.cnbv.gob.mx/dnoticia/basededatosinclusionfinanciera2024',date:'2024'}],
    method:`Counts lead this page because peso totals are dominated by large transfers. Each slot keeps its own observation date. ${debitReview?'Banxico is reviewing its series for the peso value of debit-card purchases after a jump far outside its history; those pesos are excluded until the review ends.':''} The in-store-to-online ratio compares the same quarter for both series, and every year-over-year figure here compares the same month or the same quarter one year earlier. Credit-card purchases are derived by subtracting debit purchases from the debit-plus-credit total for the same quarter. Peso figures are not adjusted for inflation. The walkthrough above carries its revision date on the page.`
  };
}

function politicsTopic(){
  // ON THE ANATOMY (Fable build spec 2026-08-01). The data-poor pole with a rule
  // attached: no polls, no seat math, no forecasts — but official election
  // results are data, so the chamber composition is a legal, necessary table.
  // Two table slots, no charts, and the page says why.
  const today=new Date();today.setUTCHours(0,0,0,0);
  const official=DB.events.filter(e=>e.date&&dateObj(e.date)>=today&&e.sourceUrl&&(e.kind==='political'||e.kind==='fiscal'||/Congress|election|electoral|budget|informe|presidential/i.test(`${e.label} ${e.mechanism||''}`))).sort((a,b)=>String(a.date).localeCompare(String(b.date)));
  if(!official.length)throw new Error('Political calendar is unavailable');
  const shortName=l=>esc(String(l).split('\u2014')[0].split('(')[0].trim());
  const REVISED='Aug 1, 2026';
  // Composition of the chamber as certified by the electoral authority after the
  // 2024 federal election. Official results, dated, with the constitutional
  // threshold marked — not seat projections.
  const CHAMBER={year:'2024',label:'INE · federal election results',url:'https://computos2024.ine.mx/votos-distrito/mapa',
    rows:[['Governing coalition, Chamber of Deputies','364 of 500 seats','2024','INE'],
          ['Governing coalition, Senate','83 of 128 seats','2024','INE'],
          ['Threshold for a constitutional amendment','two thirds of each chamber','Constitution, art. 135','DOF']]};
  return {
    anatomy:true,
    title:'Politics',
    stamp:`calendar through ${dateLabel(last(official).date)}`,
    lede:`One coalition came out of the ${CHAMBER.year} federal election with ${CHAMBER.rows[0][1]} in Mexico's Chamber of Deputies and ${CHAMBER.rows[1][1]} in the Senate. Changing the constitution here takes ${CHAMBER.rows[2][1]}, and the Chamber count clears that on its own, so a rule that would take years to move in most countries can move in Mexico inside one legislative session. The dates that session runs on are set in law and published in advance, and they are what this page tracks.`,
    howItWorks:{
      revised:REVISED,
      paras:[
        `Mexico's political year runs on a spine of dates set in law rather than custom. On September 1 the president gives an annual address to Congress that names the government's priorities. Within days the executive has to send Congress the paquete económico, the annual budget package, which puts prices on those priorities: the Ley de Ingresos, a revenue law setting what may be taxed and borrowed, and the Presupuesto de Egresos, a spending budget setting who receives the money. Both chambers vote the revenue law, with the Senate's deadline at the end of October, and the Chamber of Deputies alone votes the spending budget by mid-November. Those deadlines sit in the Constitution and in budget law, which is why a missed one is itself the news, and the calendar table below carries this cycle's dates with the article that fixes each one.`,
        `What makes this cycle unusual is arithmetic. The governing coalition holds ${CHAMBER.rows[0][1]} in the Chamber of Deputies and ${CHAMBER.rows[1][1]} in the Senate, as certified by INE, the national electoral authority, after the ${CHAMBER.year} election. A constitutional amendment needs ${CHAMBER.rows[2][1]}. The Chamber count is comfortably above two thirds, so an amendment can clear that chamber with no opposition votes at all. The Senate count is a little under two thirds, so a constitutional change still has to find a few votes there from outside the coalition. Ordinary laws, which is most of what ever reaches a company, need only a simple majority in each chamber, and the coalition is well clear of that in both.`,
        `The same arithmetic produced the judicial reform. Judges, including those on the Supreme Court, are now chosen by election rather than appointed, and the calendar table below puts the next cohort of judicial seats on the same 2027 ballot as the midterm elections. For a company, that changes who settles a dispute with the state: a judge hearing a challenge to a new rule is now elected by the same voters as the majority that wrote the rule.`,
        `This page carries no polls, no seat projections and no election forecasts, and it will not. Certified election results are data, which is why the seat table below is here. What voters intend to do next year is not data, and no honest chart of it exists. That is why this section is built from tables and dated events instead of charts.`,
        `That leaves two things to follow: the dates, and the numbers attached to them. An address can promise anything. The budget package has to say how much and to whom, Congress has to vote it by a date fixed in law, and the text is public before the vote.`
      ]
    },
    slots:[
      {table:{cols:['Measure','Value','Period','Source'],rows:CHAMBER.rows},
       read:`The third row is the bar for changing the constitution: ${CHAMBER.rows[2][1]}. The Chamber count clears it and the Senate count does not, which makes the Senate the only chamber where a constitutional change has to be negotiated. The Chamber matters twice over, because it is also the chamber that alone approves the spending budget each November. These are certified results from the ${CHAMBER.year} election rather than projections, and they hold until the seats are next contested.`},
      {table:{cols:['Date','What it settles','Source'],rows:official.slice(0,6).map(e=>[shortDate(e.date)+' '+dateObj(e.date).getUTCFullYear(),shortName(e.label),esc(e.source||'Official')])},
       read:`Each row settles one thing: what may be taxed, who receives the money, or who sits in the chamber that votes next time. For anyone buying into or lending to a Mexican company, these are the dates on which tax rules and public spending can legally change, and the source column names the law or authority that fixes each one. Nothing appears here without an original source, and no date is here on rumour.`}
    ],
    quarter:[
      `Nothing this quarter moved either number this page turns on. The coalition still holds ${CHAMBER.rows[0][1]} and ${CHAMBER.rows[1][1]} from the ${CHAMBER.year} election, and the statutory dates ahead are unchanged, starting with ${shortDate(official[0].date)} ${dateObj(official[0].date).getUTCFullYear()}. Between elections those seat counts change only by resignation or defection, and the deadlines change only by changing the law, so a quarter that moves neither is the normal case.`
    ],
    ahead:official.slice(0,5),
    view:{
      paras:[
        `I watch the deadlines here rather than the speeches, because the deadlines carry more information. A government holding ${CHAMBER.rows[0][1]} does not need the opposition to pass its ordinary agenda, so when the budget package lands late or a vote slips past its constitutional date, the delay is evidence of a fight inside the coalition rather than with the opposition. The document I read for intent is the spending budget the Chamber votes by mid-November, because it has to name amounts and recipients, while the September address only has to name priorities.`,
        `My base case for this cycle is that it runs to the calendar: the budget package delivered on its statutory date, both votes closing on time, and no constitutional change failing in the Chamber for want of numbers. The consequence I would put to anyone buying into a Mexican company is that the risk here is speed rather than deadlock. A rule that affects a business can change inside one legislative session, without opposition votes in the Chamber, and the court that would hear the challenge is now filled by election. The two documents that say what a Mexican business will be taxed and who the state will pay are the revenue law and the spending budget, both published before the votes that close by mid-November.`
      ],
      changeMind:`the governing coalition losing its two-thirds margin in the Chamber of Deputies at the 2027 midterm elections, when every seat in that chamber is on the ballot and the Senate's are not, or a statutory budget date passing unmet with no political cost. Either would mean the seat table above has stopped explaining the year, and this page would have to track bargaining and enforcement rather than dates.`
    },
    record:changedMatches('politics',3),
    sources:[{label:CHAMBER.label,href:CHAMBER.url,date:CHAMBER.year},...new Map(official.slice(0,8).map(e=>[e.source,{label:e.source,href:e.sourceUrl,date:e.date}])).values()],
    method:`Only dates tied to an original source appear here. No polling averages, no seat projections, no outcome inference: certified results from the electoral authority are data and are shown, while anything about future voter behaviour is not. The minimum wage is set by decree and belongs to the Economy page, which is where it is charted. The walkthrough above carries its revision date on the page.`
  };
}

function societyTopic(){
  // ON THE ANATOMY (Fable build spec 2026-08-01). Two charts, two tables. The
  // labour and security tables are built from dated standing facts because
  // informality and the cost-crimes are not wired as time series.
  const rem=s('banxico-remesas'),R=last(rem),RY=yearAgo(rem,R);
  const demo=DB.demographics,vitals=DB.vitals,crime=DB.crime;
  const pop=demo?.population?.latest,popYear=demo?.latestYear,births=vitals?.births,deaths=vitals?.deaths;
  const crimeTotal=crime?.values?Object.values(crime.values).reduce((t,v)=>t+Number(v||0),0):null,crimePeriod=observationPeriod(crime?.meta);
  // RY and bFirst are required, not guarded inside the prose. The smoke test forbids a
  // conditional in the two judgment fields (Fable: judgment changes by revision, not by
  // ternary). A paragraph that drops its comparison when a lookup misses is a second
  // judgment hiding in a ternary. Missing inputs take the documented failure state.
  if(!R||!RY||!Number.isFinite(pop)||!births?.latest||!deaths?.latest||!Number.isFinite(crimeTotal))throw new Error('Society inputs are incomplete');
  const bSeries=(births.series||[]).map(p=>({date:`${p.year}-01-01`,value:p.value})),dSeries=(deaths.series||[]).map(p=>({date:`${p.year}-01-01`,value:p.value}));
  const bFirst=births.series?.[0];
  if(!bFirst)throw new Error('Society inputs are incomplete');
  const birthDrop=(1-births.latest.value/bFirst.value)*100;
  const FACTS={informal:{share:'55.2%',people:'33.4 million',unemp:'2.8%',label:'INEGI · ENOE',url:'https://www.inegi.org.mx/programas/enoe/15ymas/'},
               victim:{crimes:'33.5 million',unreported:'93.2%',year:'2024',label:'INEGI · ENVIPE',url:'https://www.inegi.org.mx/programas/envipe/2025/'}};
  const line=(name,points,color,format)=>({name,points,color,format});
  const REVISED='Aug 1, 2026';
  return {
    anatomy:true,
    title:'Security & society',
    stamp:`newest number ${dateLabel(R.date)}`,
    lede:`Mexico's unemployment rate is ${FACTS.informal.unemp}, low enough that in most of Europe it would read as a boom. It means something else here. There is no broad unemployment insurance, so losing a job is not a state a household can afford to sit in. People take informal work instead, and the survey counts them as employed. Most numbers on this page work that way. Each one records who registered, who reported, or who got counted, and reading one well means knowing who never made it into the count.`,
    howItWorks:{
      revised:REVISED,
      paras:[
        `Informality is the term for work outside the tax and social security system: no payroll record, no employer contributions, no receipt at the end of the sale. INEGI, the national statistics agency, measures it through ENOE, its household employment survey, and puts it at ${FACTS.informal.share} of all workers, about ${FACTS.informal.people} people in a country whose population is projected at ${fmt(pop/1e6,1)} million for ${popYear}. These are real jobs, worked full days. What is missing is the paperwork around them.`,
        `That missing paperwork is the constraint that keeps reappearing everywhere else in Mexico. It narrows the tax base, because income that never crosses a payroll is hard to reach. It keeps small firms out of bank credit and out of large supply chains, because a bank cannot underwrite an income it cannot document and a corporate buyer needs an invoice. And for anyone acquiring a Mexican business it turns three ordinary questions into the expensive ones: how much of the payroll is formal, how many suppliers can issue an invoice, and what it would cost to bring the rest inside the system.`,
        `Security is counted twice, by two methods that disagree by construction. SESNSP, the federal secretariat that compiles what state prosecutors log, publishes a running monthly total of reported offences. ENVIPE, INEGI's annual victimization survey, instead asks households what happened to them, and it counted ${FACTS.victim.crimes} crimes in ${FACTS.victim.year}, of which ${FACTS.victim.unreported} were never reported or never investigated. Reporting is therefore the filter on everything in the prosecutor file: when reported offences rise, that can mean more crime or more willingness to report it, and the file cannot separate the two. The crimes that decide what it costs to operate in a given city, extortion and cargo theft, sit heavily inside the unreported share.`,
        `Births and deaths look like the plain numbers on this page, and they nearly are, with one catch. They count registrations made in a given year rather than events that happened in it. That is why the birth line drops steeply at the start of the pandemic and rebounds the year after: civil registry offices closed, and those births were recorded late. It is also why the newest year here is ${births.latest.year} while everything else on the page runs closer to today. Read the direction of both lines rather than any single year, and note what they leave out. Migration, in either direction, is not counted here, so on the day births and deaths meet, migration alone decides whether the population still grows.`,
        `Remittances are money Mexicans working abroad send home. Banxico, Mexico's central bank, counts them in dollars, and the households that receive them spend in pesos, so two separate things decide what a transfer is worth at a checkout: the dollar amount sent, and the exchange rate on the day it lands. A month when the dollar total rises and the peso strengthens at the same time can leave a receiving household with less to spend, not more.`,
        `One reading rule for this page. Every number here carries a different vintage, from a monthly dollar flow to a registration year that closed in ${births.latest.year} and a survey that reports once a year. They describe different moments, so any comparison that lines two of them up as one quarter is comparing different things.`
      ]
    },
    slots:[
      ...(bSeries.length>5?[{chart:{title:'Registered births and deaths per year, millions',stat:`${fmt(births.latest.value/1e6,1)}m births`,series:[line('Births',bSeries,GREEN,v=>fmt(v/1e6,2)+'m'),line('Deaths',dSeries,INK,v=>fmt(v/1e6,2)+'m')],yFormat:v=>fmt(v/1e6,1)+'m',label:'Registered births and deaths per year',srcHtml:`<a href="${births.sourceUrl}" target="_blank" rel="noopener">INEGI · registered births ↗</a>`},
        read:`Mexico registered ${fmt(births.latest.value/1e6,1)} million births in ${births.latest.year} against roughly ${fmt(Math.round(deaths.latest.value/1e3),0)},000 deaths, about ${fmt(births.latest.value/deaths.latest.value,1)} births for every death${bFirst?`, and the annual birth count is now ${fmt((1-births.latest.value/bFirst.value)*100,0)}% below its ${bFirst.year} level of ${fmt(bFirst.value/1e6,1)} million`:''}. Births are falling while deaths rise with an older population, so the two lines are closing from both ends. A birth counted in ${births.latest.year} becomes a worker and a consumer roughly two decades later, which is what makes this the slowest number on the page to move and the hardest to change with policy.`}]:[]),
      {table:{cols:['Measure','Value','Period','Source'],rows:[
        ['Unemployment rate',FACTS.informal.unemp,'Latest ENOE','INEGI'],
        ['Informal share of workers',FACTS.informal.share,'Latest ENOE','INEGI'],
        ['Workers in informality',FACTS.informal.people,'Latest ENOE','INEGI']
      ]},
       read:`Near-zero measured unemployment and mass informality are two views of the same labour market, one where work is easy to find and protection is not attached to it. For an employer, ${FACTS.informal.people} informal workers is a deep hiring pool with thin employment records. For a lender, it is the same people holding income that is real and undocumented. It also means the unemployment rate, at ${FACTS.informal.unemp}, is a poor gauge of the business cycle here: when demand falls, the adjustment lands on informal work and on pay rather than on the jobless count.`},
      {chart:{title:'Remittances per month, US$ billions',stat:`$${fmt(R.value/1000,1)}bn`,series:[line('Remittances',rem.slice(-72).map(p=>({date:p.date,value:p.value/1000})),GREEN,v=>'$'+fmt(v,1)+'bn')],yFormat:v=>'$'+fmt(v,1)+'bn',label:'Monthly remittance inflows',srcId:'banxico-remesas'},
       read:`Households received $${fmt(R.value/1000,1)} billion in ${dateLabel(R.date)}${RY?`, against $${fmt(RY.value/1000,1)} billion in the same month a year earlier, a change of ${signed((R.value/RY.value-1)*100,1,'%')}`:''}. The money is earned in dollars and spent in pesos, so the exchange rate decides what reaches a grocery bill: when the peso is stronger, the same dollar transfer converts into fewer pesos of groceries. For a consumer business in Mexico, this line answers to the American job market and to the exchange rate before it answers to anything Mexican wages do.`},
      {table:{cols:['Measure','Value','Period','Source'],rows:[
        ['Reported offences, state prosecutors',`${fmt(crimeTotal/1e6,2)} million`,crimePeriod,'SESNSP'],
        ['Crimes counted by victimization survey',FACTS.victim.crimes,FACTS.victim.year,'INEGI ENVIPE'],
        ['Never reported or investigated',FACTS.victim.unreported,FACTS.victim.year,'INEGI ENVIPE']
      ]},
       read:`Prosecutors across Mexico logged ${fmt(crimeTotal/1e6,2)} million reported offences in ${crimePeriod}. Households told INEGI's victimization survey they had experienced ${FACTS.victim.crimes} crimes in ${FACTS.victim.year} alone, and ${FACTS.victim.unreported} of those never reached a prosecutor or were never investigated. The two rows cover different periods, so they do not subtract, and the distance between them is what the prosecutor file leaves out. Because reporting is the filter, a falling reported count can mean less crime or less reporting, and extortion and cargo theft, the crimes that decide the cost of operating in a given city, sit heavily inside the share that is never recorded.`}
    ],
    quarter:[
      `The measurable movement this quarter came from remittances and from the prosecutors' file. Households received $${fmt(R.value/1000,1)} billion in ${dateLabel(R.date)}, ${signed((R.value/RY.value-1)*100,1,'%')} against the same month a year earlier, and SESNSP's running total reached ${fmt(crimeTotal/1e6,2)} million reported offences for ${crimePeriod}. The labour and victimization figures did not move, because they come from surveys that report on their own schedule rather than every quarter.`,
      `A break in these patterns would look specific. It would be a monthly remittance print below its year-earlier level, or an informality share away from ${FACTS.informal.share} when INEGI next publishes ENOE. Neither is in this quarter's data. The birth and death counts cannot break in a quarter at all, since they arrive once a year and the newest registration year on this page is still ${births.latest.year}.`
    ],
    ahead:eventMatches(/unemployment|remittance|wage|poverty|population|migration|employment/i,5),
    view:{
      paras:[
        `The slowest line here is, I think, the one that matters most. Mexico registered ${fmt(births.latest.value/deaths.latest.value,1)} births for every death in ${births.latest.year}, and the annual birth count sits ${fmt(birthDrop,0)}% below its ${bFirst.year} level, with a median age of ${demo.medianAge} today. The population total is still rising and will keep rising for years, because births still outnumber deaths, and that total is the number here most likely to mislead. My concern is that the assumption of a young and growing workforce, which carries a lot of the long-horizon case for building in Mexico, is running on cohorts that have already been counted, and the birth line is the better test of it.`,
        `On security I would judge a city and a route rather than a country. National reporting can improve while extortion and cargo theft stay inside the ${FACTS.victim.unreported} that never reaches a prosecutor, and I expect that gap to hold, because the survey measures what happened to people while the prosecutor file measures what someone decided to report. For a buyer or a lender, a national homicide rate cannot answer the question that matters. The check worth making is SESNSP's municipal file, published every month, for the exact municipalities where the company keeps its plants, its routes and its stores, read next to what that company already spends on security and insurance.`
      ],
      changeMind:`informality falling decisively below 50% of workers in INEGI's ENOE survey for four consecutive quarters, or reported extortion falling in step with homicide in the monthly SESNSP file for two years. Sustained formalization would mean verifiable income is becoming ordinary here, and every consequence on this page would loosen with it. Extortion falling with homicide would mean the security improvement has finally reached the crimes that actually price risk for a business.`
    },
    record:changedMatches('society',3),
    sources:[topicSource('banxico-remesas'),{label:'CONAPO · population projections',href:demo.sourceUrl,date:String(popYear)},{label:'INEGI · registered births',href:births.sourceUrl,date:String(births.latest.year)},{label:'INEGI · registered deaths',href:deaths.sourceUrl,date:String(deaths.latest.year)},{label:'SESNSP · municipal reported offenses',href:crime.meta.sourceUrl,date:crimePeriod},{label:FACTS.informal.label,href:FACTS.informal.url,date:'ENOE'},{label:FACTS.victim.label,href:FACTS.victim.url,date:FACTS.victim.year}],
    method:`Population is a projection (${fmt(pop/1e6,1)} million for ${popYear}, median age ${demo.medianAge}); births and deaths are registrations; the security figure is a year-to-date count of reported offences. Informality, the unemployment rate and the victimization figures are dated survey results carried as standing facts, not wired time series, so they update on the survey's schedule rather than the site's. The poverty map lives in the Atlas and the real wage on the Economy page. The walkthrough above is re-read and re-stamped every quarter.`
  };
}

function usMexicoTopic(){
  // ON THE ANATOMY (Fable build spec 2026-08-01). Absorbs the retired trade page:
  // export composition becomes slot 3, and the imported-parts mechanic moves into
  // the walkthrough. Census owns the bilateral ledger; Banxico owns Mexico's
  // national accounts; the two are never spliced.
  const tu=DB.tradeUS||{},imports=(tu.series?.imports||[]).map(p=>({date:p.month+'-01',value:p.value})),exports=(tu.series?.exports||[]).map(p=>({date:p.month+'-01',value:p.value})),L=tu.latest||{};
  const partners=DB.partners?.exports?.items||[],us=partners.find(x=>x.name==='USA'),partnerYear=Number(DB.partners?.referenceYear||DB.partners?.year);
  // Year-ago prints are required rather than guarded inside the prose. The smoke test
  // forbids a conditional in the two judgment fields (Fable: judgment changes by revision,
  // not by ternary). A sentence that quietly drops its comparison is a different claim
  // from the one that keeps it. Missing inputs take the documented failure state.
  const impYr=yearAgo(imports),expYr=yearAgo(exports);
  if(!L.month||!Number.isFinite(Number(L.twoway_bn))||!imports.length||!exports.length||!us||!Number.isFinite(partnerYear)||!impYr||!expYr)throw new Error('U.S.-Mexico inputs are incomplete');
  const tradeMonth=L.month+'-01';
  const censusMexicoUrl='https://www.census.gov/foreign-trade/balance/c2010.html';
  const comtradeUrl='https://comtradeplus.un.org/TradeFlow/';
  const comp=compositionEvidence(DB.products);
  const next=eventMatches(/USMCA|U.S.|US-Mexico|tariff|trade talks|border|bilateral/i,6,/Presupuesto|Paquete|Ley de Ingresos|foreign direct investment|IED/i);
  const line=(name,points,color,format)=>({name,points,color,format});
  const REVISED='Aug 1, 2026';
  return {
    anatomy:true,
    title:'US & Mexico',
    stamp:`newest number ${dateLabel(tradeMonth)}`,
    lede:`Mexico sent ${fmt(us.share,0)}% of its goods exports to the United States in ${partnerYear}, and the next-largest destination, ${esc(partners[1].name)}, took ${fmt(partners[1].share,1)}%. One customer at that scale sets the terms of everything else: what Mexican factories build, how many people they employ, and how much a rule change abroad can cost them. The rulebook covering that trade, USMCA, the agreement among the United States, Mexico and Canada, is now reviewed every twelve months rather than settled for sixteen years at a time.`,
    howItWorks:{
      revised:REVISED,
      paras:[
        `In ${dateLabel(tradeMonth)} the United States bought $${fmt(L.imports_bn,1)} billion of Mexican goods and sold $${fmt(L.exports_bn,1)} billion back, which left Mexico about <b>$${fmt(Math.abs(L.balance_bn),1)} billion</b> ahead in a single month. Mexico ends every month ahead. A surplus that size normally reads as the stronger hand, and it is not one here, because the money and the dependence point in opposite directions. Mexico has one realistic customer for factory output at scale, and the United States has dozens of suppliers of which Mexico is one. Every tariff round is argued inside that gap.`,
        `What crosses the border is mostly factory output, and much of it crosses twice.${tu.topExports?.find(x=>x.hs==='84')&&tu.topImports?.find(x=>x.hs==='84')&&tu.topExports?.find(x=>x.hs==='85')&&tu.topImports?.find(x=>x.hs==='85')?` Over the last twelve months the United States sold Mexico $${fmt(tu.topExports.find(x=>x.hs==='84').value_bn,1)} billion of machinery and computer equipment and bought $${fmt(tu.topImports.find(x=>x.hs==='84').value_bn,1)} billion of the same category back (U.S. Census), and sold $${fmt(tu.topExports.find(x=>x.hs==='85').value_bn,1)} billion of electrical goods and electronics against $${fmt(tu.topImports.find(x=>x.hs==='85').value_bn,1)} billion coming north.`:''} Those are the same product families travelling in both directions, because a large part of Mexican manufacturing is assembly: components arrive from the north, workers add value, and the finished machine or vehicle ships back. So a tariff on a car assembled in Mexico lands partly on American parts that already crossed the border once, and it reaches a Mexican payroll through a supply chain rather than through a customs line.${tu.topExports?.find(x=>x.hs==='27')&&tu.topImports?.find(x=>x.hs==='27')?` Oil moves the other way. The United States sold Mexico $${fmt(tu.topExports.find(x=>x.hs==='27').value_bn,1)} billion of fuels and oil over the same twelve months and bought $${fmt(tu.topImports.find(x=>x.hs==='27').value_bn,1)} billion back, because Mexico exports crude and buys much of the gasoline and diesel it burns from American refineries.`:''}`,
        `The rulebook now runs on a twelve-month clock. USMCA came with an option to lock its terms in for another sixteen years. That option was not taken. What replaced it is a joint review every twelve months, running until the three countries agree an extension or the calendar reaches 2036. Each round can reopen tariffs and the regional content rules, which set how much of a car or an appliance has to be made inside North America to cross the border duty free. A factory pays for itself over ten or twenty years, so a plant decision taken today is a bet on terms that are guaranteed for one more year.`,
        `China is in the room without being at the table.${DB.partners?.imports?.items?.find(x=>x.name==='China')&&partners.find(x=>x.name==='China')?` In ${partnerYear} China supplied ${fmt(DB.partners.imports.items.find(x=>x.name==='China').share,1)}% of everything Mexico imported and bought ${fmt(partners.find(x=>x.name==='China').share,1)}% of what Mexico exported (UN COMTRADE).`:''} Mexican plants buy Chinese components and sell American finished goods, and because US tariffs on Chinese imports are high, Mexico is the cheapest legal door into the North American market. Policing that door is what the regional content rules and the annual review are for. Chinese investment in Mexican plants and American accusations of transshipment, meaning Chinese goods lightly reworked in Mexico to claim North American origin, are the same argument seen from two sides.`,
        `One reading rule. Use one country's customs data at a time. Mexico and the United States both publish this trade and their totals never match, because each side counts a shipment at its own border, values it its own way and stamps it with its own date. This page uses U.S. Census figures for the monthly bilateral ledger and the reconciled UN COMTRADE file for annual export shares, and never adds the two together. That is also why the dates differ: the concentration figures here carry ${partnerYear} and the trade figures carry ${dateLabel(tradeMonth)}.`
      ]
    },
    slots:[
      {chart:{title:'US goods trade with Mexico, monthly, US$ billions',stat:`$${fmt(L.twoway_bn,1)}bn`,series:[line('US buys from Mexico',imports,GREEN,v=>'$'+fmt(v,1)+'bn'),line('US sells to Mexico',exports,INK,v=>'$'+fmt(v,1)+'bn')],yFormat:v=>'$'+fmt(v,0),label:'Monthly US imports from and exports to Mexico',srcHtml:`<a href="${censusMexicoUrl}" target="_blank" rel="noopener">U.S. Census · trade with Mexico ↗</a>`},
       read:`Mexico sold the United States $${fmt(L.imports_bn,1)} billion of goods in ${dateLabel(tradeMonth)} and bought $${fmt(L.exports_bn,1)} billion back${yearAgo(imports)?`, against $${fmt(yearAgo(imports).value,1)} billion sold in the same month a year earlier`:''}. That is about $${fmt(L.twoway_bn/30,1)} billion crossing in both directions every day. The two lines rise and fall together, which is what a shared production line looks like from the outside: when American demand rises, Mexican plants buy more American parts to meet it.`},
      {table:{cols:['Measure','Value','Period','Source'],rows:[
        ['Share of Mexican goods exports going to the US',`${fmt(us.share,0)}%`,String(partnerYear),'UN COMTRADE'],
        ['Monthly two-way goods trade',`$${fmt(L.twoway_bn,1)}bn`,dateLabel(tradeMonth),'U.S. Census']
      ]},
       read:`These two rows are the negotiating position. The share in the first row is the share of Mexican export income that has nowhere else of that size to go, while the United States in the same disruption would be losing one supplier among many. For a company operating here, the concentration is a revenue question first: a Mexican manufacturer's order book is largely a bet on American factory and consumer demand, however the tariff argument ends.`},
      ...(comp?[{chart:{title:comp.title,stat:'',visual:comp.visual,legend:comp.legend,table:comp.table,label:'Mexico goods exports by product group',srcHtml:`<a href="${comtradeUrl}" target="_blank" rel="noopener">UN COMTRADE · HS 2-digit exports ↗</a> · ${partnerYear}`,css:'composition-evidence'},
       read:`Vehicles, machinery and electronics carry most of the export dollars, so Mexican export income tracks American factory and consumer demand more closely than it tracks anything happening in Mexico. Those are also the categories the annual review keeps reopening, so the exposure and the tariff risk sit in the same place. Fruit, beverages and medical instruments are real export businesses here too, and they sit under different tariff lines, so how exposed a company is to a review round depends on which box it sells from.`}]:[])
    ],
    quarter:[
      `Trade volume rose against a year earlier. In ${dateLabel(tradeMonth)} the United States bought $${fmt(L.imports_bn,1)} billion of Mexican goods, against $${fmt(impYr.value,1)} billion in the same month a year earlier, and sold $${fmt(L.exports_bn,1)} billion back, against $${fmt(expYr.value,1)} billion. Two-way trade came to $${fmt(L.twoway_bn,1)} billion in the month, and Mexico's surplus widened to about $${fmt(Math.abs(L.balance_bn),1)} billion.`,
      `The structure did not move with the volume. The export-share and composition figures come from an annual file whose reference year is still ${partnerYear}, so the concentration cannot have changed in the data yet, and the treaty is still on its annual review clock, with no long extension agreed. The U.S. Census ledger updates monthly and is where a change in direction would show up first.`
    ],
    ahead:next.length?next.slice(0,5):[
      {date:'Monthly',label:'U.S. Census goods trade with Mexico',source:'U.S. Census Bureau'},
      {date:'Annual',label:'USMCA joint review round',source:'USTR · Secretaría de Economía'},
      {date:'Oct 2026',label:'Next full review of this section',source:'The Mexico Brief'}
    ],
    view:{
      paras:[
        `I put less weight on the monthly surplus than on anything else here. Mexico ends every month ahead, currently by about $${fmt(Math.abs(L.balance_bn),1)} billion, and still negotiates from the weaker side, because ${fmt(us.share,0)}% of its export income sits with a buyer that has other places to shop. So I read tariff headlines through the concentration and the product mix rather than through who is running a deficit. A tariff round argued as policy in Washington arrives as a payroll question in Monterrey.`,
        `I doubt these annual reviews produce either a grand bargain or a rupture. A twelve-month format rewards deferring anything hard to the next round, so narrow sector-by-sector outcomes are what I would plan around. Declining the sixteen-year extension left the market open and the horizon short, and I think the horizon is the more expensive loss, because capital allocation runs on horizons before it runs on tariff rates. For a single Mexican company, two shares say more than the national balance does: how much of its revenue ends up in the United States, and how much of its inputs come from Asia. American revenue is what a demand shock travels through, Asian inputs are what a content rule travels through, and the national trade balance shows neither.`
      ],
      changeMind:`the export share to the United States falling below about 75% for two consecutive reference years in the UN COMTRADE partner file, which updates once a year, or two annual review rounds closing with tariffs and content rules untouched. A falling share would mean the exposure is genuinely diversifying. Two quiet rounds would mean the annual clock has stopped repricing the horizon, and I would stop treating the review calendar as the main risk on this page.`
    },
    record:changedMatches('usmexico',3),
    sources:[{label:'U.S. Census · trade with Mexico',href:censusMexicoUrl,date:tu.meta?.latestMonth},{label:'UN COMTRADE · export exposure and composition',href:comtradeUrl,date:String(partnerYear)}],
    method:`The bilateral ledger uses U.S. Census throughout; Banxico owns Mexico's national accounts and the two are never spliced. The export-share and composition figures come from the reconciled COMTRADE partner file for ${partnerYear}. Goods only: services, profit and the share of value actually created in Mexico are not in these numbers. The walkthrough above carries its revision date on the page.`
  };
}

const TOPIC_FEED={economy:'economy',payments:'payments',politics:'politics',society:'society',usmexico:'usmexico'};
const BUILDERS={economy:economyTopic,payments:paymentsTopic,politics:politicsTopic,society:societyTopic,usmexico:usMexicoTopic};
let current=ROUTE_TOPIC;
function validTopic(){return BUILDERS[ROUTE_TOPIC]?ROUTE_TOPIC:'economy';}
function renderSources(t){return `<div class="source-list">${t.sources.map(x=>`<div><a href="${x.href}" target="_blank" rel="noopener">${x.label} ↗</a>${x.date?` · observation ${x.date}`:''}</div>`).join('')}</div><p>${t.method}</p>`;}
function evidenceBlock(e,id,label='In the data'){
  if(!e)return '';
  const titleId=id+'-title',visualId=id+'-visual',tableId=id+'-table';
  return `<section class="section ${e.css||''}" id="${id}" aria-labelledby="${titleId}"><div class="section-head"><div><div class="mono-k">${label}</div><h2 id="${titleId}">${e.k}</h2></div></div><article class="evidence-shell"><header class="evidence-head"><div><h3>${e.title}</h3><div class="evidence-sub">${e.sub}</div></div><div class="view-toggle" role="group" aria-label="Choose ${e.k} view"><button type="button" class="active" aria-pressed="true" data-evidence-for="${id}" data-evidence="visual">${e.visualLabel||'Chart'}</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="false" data-evidence-for="${id}" data-evidence="table">${e.tableLabel||'Table'}</button></div></header><div class="evidence-body" id="${visualId}">${e.legend?`<div class="chart-legend">${e.legend}</div>`:''}<div class="chart-wrap">${e.visual}</div><div class="chart-note">${e.note}${evidenceSources(e.sources)}</div></div><div class="table-wrap" id="${tableId}">${e.table}</div><div class="evidence-read"><div><h4>What it shows</h4><p>${e.read[0]}</p></div><div><h4>What it does not show</h4><p>${e.read[1]}</p></div></div></article></section>`;
}
// The chart card, stripped to four elements (Fable restart 2026-07-20): a plain title with
// units folded in, the current value, the chart with its crosshair, and ONE footer line
// carrying provenance and the table toggle. Everything else the old card wore is gone.
function chartSrcLine(c){
  if(c.srcHtml)return c.srcHtml;
  const ids=[c.srcId,c.srcId2].filter(Boolean);
  return ids.map(id=>`<a href="${sourceUrl(id)}" target="_blank" rel="noopener">${sourceName(id)}${seriesId(id)?` · ${seriesId(id)}`:''} ↗</a>`).join(' · ')+(ids.length?` · through ${dateLabel(obs(ids[0]))}`:'');
}
function chartShell(c,id){
  const visual=c.visual||chartSVG({series:c.series,yFormat:c.yFormat,label:c.label,band:c.band||null});
  const table=c.table||dataTable(c.series,24);
  return `<article class="chart-card ${c.css||''}"><header class="chart-head"><h3>${c.title}</h3>${c.stat?`<div class="chart-stat">${c.stat}</div>`:''}</header><div class="evidence-body" id="${id}-visual">${c.visual?(c.legend||''):`<div class="chart-legend">${legend(c.series)}</div>`}<div class="chart-wrap">${visual}</div></div><div class="table-wrap" id="${id}-table">${table}</div><div class="chart-foot"><span>${chartSrcLine(c)}</span><button type="button" class="tbl-toggle" data-evidence-for="${id}" aria-pressed="false">Table</button></div></article>`;
}
// The crosshair (Alan 2026-07-20): pointer over any chart snaps to the nearest observation,
// draws a vertical guide and a dot per series, and shows a tooltip with the date and each
// pre-formatted value. pointerdown covers tap-to-inspect on phones. Delegated once on the
// document, so it survives every re-render and needs no per-chart wiring.
(function(){
  function clearHover(holder){const tip=holder.querySelector('.chart-tip');if(tip)tip.hidden=true;holder.querySelectorAll('svg .hv').forEach(g=>{g.innerHTML='';});}
  function onPoint(ev){
    const svg=ev.target.closest?.('.chart-hover svg');
    if(!svg)return;
    const holder=svg.closest('.chart-hover');
    const data=holder._hd||(holder._hd=JSON.parse(holder.dataset.series));
    const a=svg.dataset,W=+a.w,H=+a.h,L=+a.l,R=+a.r,T=+a.t,B=+a.b,x0=+a.x0,x1=+a.x1,y0=+a.y0,y1=+a.y1;
    const rect=svg.getBoundingClientRect();
    const xv=(ev.clientX-rect.left)/rect.width*W;
    if(xv<L-6||xv>W-R+6){clearHover(holder);return;}
    const tt=x0+(Math.min(Math.max(xv,L),W-R)-L)/(W-L-R||1)*(x1-x0||1);
    const rows=[];let snap=null,snapD=Infinity;
    for(const s of data){let best=null,bd=Infinity;for(const p of s.p){const d=Math.abs(p[0]-tt);if(d<bd){bd=d;best=p;}}if(!best)continue;rows.push({s,p:best});if(bd<snapD){snapD=bd;snap=best;}}
    if(!rows.length)return;
    const X=v=>L+(v-x0)/(x1-x0||1)*(W-L-R),Y=v=>T+(y1-v)/(y1-y0||1)*(H-T-B);
    const g=svg.querySelector('.hv');
    const cx=X(snap[0]).toFixed(1);
    g.innerHTML=`<line class="hv-line" x1="${cx}" x2="${cx}" y1="${T}" y2="${H-B}"/>`+rows.map(r=>`<circle class="hv-dot" cx="${X(r.p[0]).toFixed(1)}" cy="${Y(r.p[1]).toFixed(1)}" r="4.5" fill="${r.s.c}"/>`).join('');
    let tip=holder.querySelector('.chart-tip');
    if(!tip){tip=document.createElement('div');tip.className='chart-tip';holder.appendChild(tip);}
    tip.hidden=false;
    tip.innerHTML=`<div class="tip-d">${esc(snap[3])}</div>`+rows.map(r=>`<div class="tip-r"><i style="background:${r.s.c}"></i><span>${esc(r.s.n)}</span><b>${esc(r.p[2])}</b></div>`).join('');
    const hr=holder.getBoundingClientRect();
    let leftPx=ev.clientX-hr.left+16;const tw=tip.offsetWidth||170;
    if(leftPx+tw>hr.width-4)leftPx=Math.max(4,ev.clientX-hr.left-tw-16);
    tip.style.left=leftPx+'px';
    tip.style.top=Math.max(0,ev.clientY-hr.top-tip.offsetHeight-14)+'px';
  }
  document.addEventListener?.('pointermove',onPoint);
  document.addEventListener?.('pointerdown',onPoint);
  document.addEventListener?.('pointerout',(ev)=>{const holder=ev.target.closest?.('.chart-hover');if(holder&&!(ev.relatedTarget&&holder.contains(ev.relatedTarget)))clearHover(holder);});
})();
// ============ THE SECTION ANATOMY (Fable ruling 2026-08-01) ============
// Every section page meets the reader in the same order: lede → How it works →
// The numbers → What changed this quarter → What's ahead → My view → The record
// → Sources and method. The walkthrough carries its own revised date; every
// number slot carries a read; a sourced table is a valid slot, so a data-poor
// section fills the same shape without faking depth. This supersedes the
// 2026-07-20 letter ruling as the page's organizing idea; the honesty machinery
// (stamps, labeled opinion, falsifiable view, dated sources) is unchanged.
function slotTable(t){
  return `<div class="slot-table"><table class="mck"><thead><tr>${t.cols.map(c=>`<th>${c}</th>`).join('')}</tr></thead><tbody>${t.rows.map(r=>`<tr>${r.map((cell,i)=>`<td${i?'':' class="rowhead"'}>${cell}</td>`).join('')}</tr>`).join('')}</tbody></table></div>`;
}
function renderAnatomy(t){
  const slots=t.slots.map((slot,i)=>`<div class="slot">${slot.chart?chartShell(slot.chart,'slot-'+i):slotTable(slot.table)}<p class="slot-read">${slot.read}</p></div>`).join('');
  return `
    <section class="topic-hero anatomy-page"><div><div class="edition-label">Quarterly review</div><h1>${t.title}</h1><div class="edition-meta">${EDITION.label} · Published ${EDITION.published} · Data through ${EDITION.dataThrough} · Next review ${EDITION.next}</div><div class="edition-contract">The figures are sourced. The argument and forecasts are mine.</div></div><div class="topic-asof story-stamp">${t.stamp}</div></section>
    <p class="lead">${t.lede}</p>
    <section class="section"><div class="section-head"><div><h2>How it works</h2></div><div class="section-note">Last revised ${t.howItWorks.revised}</div></div>${t.howItWorks.paras.map(x=>`<p class="story-p">${x}</p>`).join('')}</section>
    <section class="section"><h2>The numbers</h2>${slots}</section>
    <section class="section"><h2>What changed this quarter</h2>${t.quarter.map(x=>`<p class="story-p">${x}</p>`).join('')}</section>
    <section class="section"><h2>What's ahead</h2>${upcomingList(t.ahead)}</section>
    <section class="section"><h2>My view</h2>${t.view.paras.map(x=>`<p class="story-p">${x}</p>`).join('')}<p class="story-p change-mind"><b>What would change my mind:</b> ${t.view.changeMind}</p></section>
    <section class="section"><h2>The record</h2>${updateList(t.record)}</section>
    <p class="daily-link-row"><a class="daily-link" href="/?topic=${encodeURIComponent(TOPIC_FEED[current]||'')}#all-news">Daily coverage of this section lives on the homepage →</a></p>
    <details class="sources"><summary>Sources and method</summary><div class="sources-body">${renderSources(t)}</div></details>`;
}
function render(topic){
  current=topic;let t;
  try{t=BUILDERS[topic]();}
  catch(error){
    console.error(error);window.reportMexicoDataError?.(`topic:${topic}`);
    $('#topicApp').className='';
    $('#topicApp').innerHTML=`<section class="topic-hero"><div><h1>${LABELS[topic]}</h1><p class="topic-scope">This page is waiting for its required source data.</p></div></section><section class="now"><div class="mono-k">Data check</div><div><h2>This page couldn't load its source data. Try again, or check feed status.</h2><p><a href="/sources.html#livenow">Open feed status →</a></p></div></section>`;
    return;
  }
  $$('.topic-switch a').forEach(b=>b.classList.toggle('active',b.dataset.topic===topic));$('.topic-select').value=topic;
  // One argument with receipts (Fable restart 2026-07-20): lead thesis, single Next line,
  // 2-4 claim sections whose LAST sentence carries the leverage, stripped chart cards,
  // then the record, the calendar, and collapsed sources.
  $('#topicApp').className='';
  if(t.anatomy){
    $('#topicApp').innerHTML=renderAnatomy(t);
    $$('.tbl-toggle').forEach(b=>b.addEventListener('click',()=>{const id=b.dataset.evidenceFor,showTable=b.getAttribute('aria-pressed')!=='true';b.setAttribute('aria-pressed',String(showTable));b.textContent=showTable?'Chart':'Table';$('#'+id+'-visual').classList.toggle('hidden',showTable);$('#'+id+'-table').classList.toggle('open',showTable)}));
    return;
  }
  $('#topicApp').innerHTML=`
    <section class="topic-hero"><div><h1>${t.title}</h1></div><div class="topic-asof story-stamp">${t.asof}<br><a class="daily-link" href="/?topic=${encodeURIComponent(TOPIC_FEED[current]||'')}#all-news">Daily coverage of this section lives on the homepage →</a></div></section>
    <p class="lead">${t.lead}</p>
    ${t.next?`<div class="next-line"><span class="nk">Next</span>${t.next}</div>`:''}
    ${t.sections.map((sec,i)=>`<section class="section story-sec"><h2>${String(sec.h2).replace(/\.\s*$/,'')}</h2>${sec.prose.map(p=>`<p class="story-p">${p}</p>`).join('')}${sec.chart?chartShell(sec.chart,'story-ev-'+i):''}</section>`).join('')}
    <section class="section"><div class="changes-grid"><div><div class="section-head"><div><div class="mono-k">The record</div><h2>What's moving</h2></div></div>${updateList(t.updates)}</div><aside><div class="section-head"><div><div class="mono-k">The calendar</div><h2>What could change this page</h2></div></div>${upcomingList(t.upcoming)}</aside></div></section>
    <details class="sources"><summary>Sources and method</summary><div class="sources-body">${renderSources(t)}</div></details>`;
  $$('.tbl-toggle').forEach(b=>b.addEventListener('click',()=>{const id=b.dataset.evidenceFor,showTable=b.getAttribute('aria-pressed')!=='true';b.setAttribute('aria-pressed',String(showTable));b.textContent=showTable?'Chart':'Table';$('#'+id+'-visual').classList.toggle('hidden',showTable);$('#'+id+'-table').classList.toggle('open',showTable)}));
}
$('.topic-switch').innerHTML=TOPIC_ORDER.map(t=>`<a href="${ROUTES[t]}" data-topic="${t}"${t===validTopic()?' aria-current="page"':''}>${LABELS[t]}</a>`).join('');
$('.topic-select').innerHTML=TOPIC_ORDER.map(t=>`<option value="${t}">${LABELS[t]}</option>`).join('');
$('.topic-select').addEventListener('change',e=>{location.href=ROUTES[e.target.value]});
render(validTopic());
</script>
